    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>No. No randomised trial has ever measured stretch marks after an oral collagen supplement, so there is no evidence that collagen fades or prevents them.</strong> Collagen peptide trials measure firmness, elasticity and hydration: a 2025 trial in 85 women found 12.2% more firmness at 84 days, but never looked at a single stretch mark.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Zero direct evidence:</strong> a PubMed search for oral collagen and striae distensae returns no randomised trial with stretch marks as an outcome.</li>
        <li><strong>Creams do not prevent them either:</strong> the Cochrane review pooled 6 trials and 800 pregnant women and found no significant difference against placebo.</li>
        <li><strong>What does have evidence:</strong> topical tretinoin plus bipolar radiofrequency ranked first across 14 trials and 651 patients, with fractional CO2 laser the best single laser.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limit:</strong> even the best studied options give partial fading, not removal, and work better on new red marks than on old white ones.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

