      <h2 id="what-trials-measure">What do collagen trials actually measure?</h2>
      <p>They measure skin properties, not skin marks. That distinction is the whole article.</p>
      <p>The most recent good example is a double-blind, placebo-controlled trial published in the <em>Journal of Medicinal Food</em> in 2025. Eighty-five women aged 45 to 60 took 2.5g of collagen oligopeptides daily for 84 days. Firmness rose roughly 3.7% at day 28, 9.7% at day 56 and 12.2% at day 84, and elasticity rose about 3.1%, 6.8% and 8.3% at the same points.</p>
      <p>Two honest caveats belong with those numbers. First, firmness also improved significantly in the placebo group by day 84, which is a reminder that measured skin properties drift for all sorts of reasons over three months. Second, and more important here: hydration, firmness and elasticity are instrument readings taken on normal skin. A stretch mark is a defined lesion with its own structure. Improving the average elasticity of a forearm tells you nothing about whether a silver line on a thigh looks different. We cover what collagen peptides do move in <a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">collagen for skin</a>, and the same endpoint problem appears again in <a href="/pages/collagen-for-loose-skin">collagen for loose skin after weight loss</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="why-hard">Why are stretch marks so hard to shift?</h2>
      <p>Because a mature stretch mark is scar tissue, and because the main driver appears to be inherited rather than behavioural.</p>
      <p>Stretch marks pass through two stages. New ones, striae rubra, are red or purple and inflamed, with mast cell degranulation and macrophage activity breaking down elastic fibres in the mid-dermis. Over months they mature into striae alba: pale, slightly sunken, scar-like and permanent in structure. Reviews of the pathogenesis agree that the mechanism is not fully settled but centres on the skin's extracellular matrix, particularly fibrillin, elastin and collagen, with cortisol and adrenocorticotropic hormone altering how fibroblasts behave.</p>
      <p>The genetic signal is strong. A genome-wide association analysis by Tung and colleagues, summarised in the Medscape clinical review of striae distensae, implicated elastic microfibrils in non-syndromic stretch marks. Family history is one of the most consistent risk factors across pregnancy studies, and one pregnancy cohort found no association between how much the abdomen actually stretched and how many stretch marks appeared, which is an awkward result for anyone selling prevention.</p>
      <p>What does raise risk, consistently: higher pre-pregnancy BMI, with one study finding women at a BMI of 25 or above about five times more likely to develop striae than women at 18.4, and gestational weight gain, with a United States cohort reporting roughly 13% higher odds per additional kilogram. Younger maternal age and first pregnancy also recur across studies.</p>

      <h2 id="creams-pregnancy">Do creams prevent stretch marks in pregnancy?</h2>
      <p>On the best available evidence, no, and that includes creams containing collagen.</p>
      <p>The Cochrane review by Brennan, Young and Devane, <em>Topical preparations for preventing stretch marks in pregnancy</em>, pooled six trials and about 800 women. There was no statistically significant difference in whether stretch marks developed between an active topical product and placebo or no treatment, with an average risk ratio of 0.74. Three trials in 461 women looked at severity and found no clear difference. The authors concluded there was no high-quality evidence supporting any of the preparations tested, which included Alphastria, Trofolastin, Verum, olive oil and cocoa butter.</p>
      <p>One signal inside that review is worth stating fairly. An 80-woman trial of Trofolastin, a cream containing <em>Centella asiatica</em> extract, alpha tocopherol and collagen-elastin hydrolysates applied with massage, found fewer women developing stretch marks, with a confidence interval of 0.17 to 0.99 that only just clears no effect. A 2015 review in the <em>British Journal of Dermatology</em> reached a similar verdict: limited evidence for centella, possibly for massage with bitter almond oil, and weak evidence for hyaluronic acid. The hyaluronic acid trial in the Cochrane set compared the ointment against no treatment rather than placebo, so part of any benefit may simply be the massage. Oral hyaluronic acid is a separate question, and we have reviewed it in <a href="/pages/oral-hyaluronic-acid">oral hyaluronic acid</a>.</p>

