      <h2 id="what-works">What actually works on stretch marks you already have?</h2>
      <p>Procedures and prescription retinoids, in roughly this order, and none of them removes a stretch mark.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Topical tretinoin plus bipolar radiofrequency.</strong> In a 2020 Bayesian network meta-analysis in <em>Medicine (Baltimore)</em> covering 14 trials and 651 patients, this combination had the highest probability of being the best option for both clinical effectiveness and patient satisfaction. Radiofrequency alone ranked second.</li>
        <li><strong>Fractional CO2 laser.</strong> The best performing laser in the same analysis, and suggested as an alternative to the top combination. Small studies of fractional CO2 or radiofrequency report moderate improvement in about half of patients and marked improvement in roughly a fifth.</li>
        <li><strong>Microneedling.</strong> A 2024 systematic review and meta-analysis in <em>Aesthetic Plastic Surgery</em> found initial evidence of efficacy and safety, broadly comparable with lasers in one subgroup, with a lower rate of post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation but significantly more pain. The lower pigmentation risk matters in Indian skin, where darkening after a procedure is the most common complaint.</li>
        <li><strong>Topical tretinoin on its own.</strong> A 2025 systematic review of 69 studies found tretinoin 0.1% beneficial in most of them, with the clearest effect on recent marks. In the network meta-analysis, tretinoin alone ranked last of the options compared, so treat it as a starting point rather than the answer. It is a prescription medicine and is not used in pregnancy.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Two practical consequences. Treat early if you are going to treat at all, because red marks respond better than white ones. And expect partial fading: every one of these studies measures degrees of improvement, and the honest framing in clinic is that a treated stretch mark becomes less noticeable, not absent.</p>

      <h2 id="steroid-creams">Stretch marks from steroid creams: an Indian problem worth naming</h2>
      <p>If stretch marks have appeared somewhere that has not stretched, a cream may be the cause, and in India that cream is often a steroid.</p>
      <p>Potent topical corticosteroids such as betamethasone valerate are widely sold over the counter and widely used as fairness, anti-acne or general-purpose creams. A prospective multicentre study of dermatology outpatients published in the <em>Indian Journal of Dermatology, Venereology and Leprology</em> found use as a fairness cream to be the commonest reason patients were applying them, and that none of the 126 patients using them that way had been prescribed them by a doctor. Atrophic striae on the face were seen in 3% of patients in that cohort; prolonged use elsewhere on the body, including on misdiagnosed fungal infections, is a well-documented cause of striae and skin atrophy. The Indian Association of Dermatologists, Venereologists and Leprologists set up a task force, ITATSA, which has petitioned the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare and CDSCO about over-the-counter sale.</p>
      <p>No supplement helps this. Stopping the cream, with dermatological supervision because withdrawal can flare, is the intervention.</p>

