      <h2 id="where-it-sits">Where a collagen shot honestly sits</h2>
      <p>Not in the stretch mark conversation. It belongs in the hydration, elasticity and tone conversation, which is a smaller claim and a better supported one.</p>
      <p>Collabloom carries 8,000mg of Japanese hydrolysed marine collagen in a 30ml shot. That is more than three times the 2.5g used in the 2025 trial above, and we would rather say plainly what that does and does not mean. A larger dose of something that has never been measured against stretch marks does not become evidence for stretch marks. What the dose is good for is the endpoints that have been measured repeatedly, and even there the effect sizes are single-digit to low-double-digit percentages over 8 to 12 weeks, which you can read about in <a href="/pages/collagen-for-skin">collagen for skin</a>.</p>
      <p>One more thing we will not sell around: the group most likely to be searching this question is pregnant or recently pregnant, and we do not recommend our products in pregnancy. There is no pregnancy-specific safety data for collagen supplements, both our products are marine, and supplements are not pre-approved for safety the way medicines are. If you are pregnant or breastfeeding, the person to ask is the clinician managing your pregnancy, not a brand. The fish-source and allergy questions are set out in <a href="/pages/collagen-side-effects">collagen side effects</a>.</p>

