      <h2 id="see-a-doctor">When to see a doctor about stretch marks</h2>
      <p>Stretch marks themselves carry no medical risk. The reason for appearing is occasionally the point.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Wide purple or blue stretch marks appearing quickly without pregnancy, rapid weight change or a growth spurt, especially alongside weight gain around the trunk, a rounder face, easy bruising or new high blood pressure. Striae are a recognised feature of Cushing's syndrome and reviews advise endocrine referral where hypercortisolism is suspected.</li>
        <li>Stretch marks where a cream has been applied, as above.</li>
        <li>Stretch marks in a child or young teenager with no growth spurt to explain them.</li>
        <li>Marks that are sore, breaking down or blistering rather than simply discoloured.</li>
        <li>Anyone considering laser, radiofrequency or microneedling, which should be assessed in person, particularly in deeper skin tones where pigmentation after a procedure is the main risk.</li>
      </ul>

