      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Can collagen get rid of stretch marks?</summary>
          <p>No. No randomised trial has measured stretch marks after an oral collagen supplement, so there is no evidence that it fades them. Established white stretch marks are scar tissue, and even the best studied procedures produce partial fading rather than removal.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does taking collagen during pregnancy prevent stretch marks?</summary>
          <p>There is no evidence that it does, and we do not recommend our products in pregnancy. No trial has tested an oral collagen supplement for preventing striae, and the Cochrane review of topical preparations found no significant prevention across six trials and 800 women. Family history and pre-pregnancy BMI are stronger predictors than anything you apply or swallow.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is the most effective treatment for stretch marks?</summary>
          <p>In a 2020 network meta-analysis of 14 trials and 651 patients, topical tretinoin combined with bipolar radiofrequency had the highest probability of being the best option, with bipolar radiofrequency alone second and fractional CO2 laser the best laser. Microneedling performs comparably to lasers with less risk of pigmentation. All of them give partial improvement.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do red stretch marks and white stretch marks respond differently?</summary>
          <p>Yes. Red or purple marks, called striae rubra, are the newer inflamed stage and respond better to treatment. White marks, striae alba, are the mature atrophic stage and are harder to improve. Tretinoin in particular shows its clearest benefit on recent marks.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does collagen cream work on stretch marks?</summary>
          <p>The Cochrane review found no high-quality evidence that any topical preparation prevents stretch marks in pregnancy, and the creams tested included one containing collagen-elastin hydrolysates. That cream, Trofolastin, showed a borderline benefit in a single 80-woman trial that has not been replicated.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much collagen should I take for skin?</summary>
          <p>Trials showing effects on skin hydration, firmness and elasticity have generally used 2.5g to 10g of hydrolysed collagen peptides daily for 8 to 12 weeks. Collabloom provides 8,000mg per 30ml shot. None of those trials measured stretch marks, so the dose is relevant to skin quality rather than to striae.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Why do I have stretch marks if I have not gained weight?</summary>
          <p>Growth spurts in adolescence, muscle gain from resistance training, potent topical steroid creams and hormonal conditions such as Cushing's syndrome all cause stretch marks without weight gain. If wide purple marks appear quickly with no obvious cause, that is worth a medical opinion rather than a supplement.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

